    "case_id": "164",
    "case_summary": "A 64-year-old woman presented with a 2-month history of right upper abdominal pain without nausea, vomiting, chills, or fever. She had no history of hepatitis, surgery, or hereditary disease, and no underlying medical conditions. On examination, she was afebrile and oriented; there was right upper abdominal tenderness without rebound or palpable masses, no hepatosplenomegaly, and negative Murphy’s sign and shifting dullness.\n\nLaboratory studies showed:  \n• AFP 2.4 µg/l (normal 0.0–7.0)  \n• CEA 11.6 µg/l (normal 0.0–5.0)  \n• CA-125 32.3 KIU/l (normal 0.0–35.0)  \n• CA19-9 228.0 KIU/l (normal 0.0–30.0)  \n• Other liver enzymes and blood counts largely within reference ranges, except WBC 11.9×10^9/l (normal 3.5–9.5×10^9/l).\n\nUltrasonography revealed a heterogeneous echoic mass near the gallbladder fossa in the right liver lobe, calcifications in the right liver, multiple gallstones with gallbladder wall thickening, and enhanced pancreatic echogenicity. Contrast-enhanced CT showed a lesion in the right hepatic lobe adjacent to the gallbladder fossa, multiple enlarged hepatic hilar lymph nodes, and findings of gallstones with cholecystitis. Liver MRI demonstrated an occupying lesion in the right lobe with mild intrahepatic bile duct dilatation, raising the possibility of intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma, along with gallstones and cholecystitis. No extrahepatic primary tumor was identified.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Primary squamous cell carcinoma of the liver\nB. Cholangiocarcinoma was considered because imaging “suggesting the possibility of intrahepatic cholangiocarcinoma” on MRI\nC. Hepatocellular carcinoma was considered because HCC “usually shows marked arterial phase enhancement … with rapid\nD. Hepatic cyst was considered because substantial necrosis in PSCCL “may resemble those of hepatic cysts,” although\nE. Metastatic tumors were considered because metastatic liver lesions “usually present as single or multiple hypodense\nF. Liver abscess was considered because on CT “a liver abscess appears as a round hypodense lesion with enhanced walls\nG. None\nH. None\n",
